---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '@/components/Breadcrumb.astro';

const revised = '2026-08-14';

const pageTitle = 'APS Research';
const pageDescription =
  'The scholarly programme through which APS claims, methods, and applications are developed, compared, tested, and made available for critical assessment.';
---

<BaseLayout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <div class="content-wrapper">
    <Breadcrumb items={[{ label: 'APS Research' }]} />

    <header class="page-header">
      <h1>APS Research</h1>
      <div class="page-revised">Revised: {revised}</div>
    </header>

    <div class="article-body">
      <p>
        APS Research is the scholarly programme through which APS claims,
        methods, and applications are developed, compared with existing work,
        tested against evidence and alternatives, and made available for
        critical assessment.
      </p>

      <p>
        It brings together peer-reviewed publications, preprints, deposited
        papers, completed APS Research, and substantive work in development
        where appropriate. Every item is identified by status so that
        completion, public availability, and external peer review are not
        confused.
      </p>

      <p>
        Research is the programme; the
        <a href="/publications/">Publications page</a> records its formally
        released scholarly outputs; and the
        <a href="/articles/">Articles collection</a> communicates the framework
        and its applications across the wider APS website.
      </p>

      <h2>Current Framework Status</h2>

      <p>
        APS is currently being investigated as a <strong>candidate general
        explanatory framework for biology</strong>. Framework-level assessment
        has established that APS is <strong>eligible for testing</strong> in
        that role.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is a bounded research status. It does not establish general
        explanatory adequacy, framework-wide explanatory superiority, multiple
        bounded explanatory contributions, supported transfer of explanatory
        contribution across biological domains, or broader explanatory scope.
        Local research results therefore retain their own evidential scope and
        are not aggregated into framework-wide validation.
      </p>

       <h2>APS Research Coverage and Evidential Status</h2>

      <p>
        This matrix distinguishes areas represented in the APS conceptual
        architecture from targets that have undergone controlled comparative
        investigation and from stronger explanatory claims that would require
        additional evidential support.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>How to read this matrix:</strong> Presence in the APS corpus
        indicates conceptual or architectural coverage, not demonstrated
        explanatory success. Controlled investigation records where a particular
        APS claim has undergone a bounded comparative test; it does not imply a
        positive result. Results apply only to the stated target. Explanatory
        contribution and transfer require separate evidential warrant and must
        not be inferred from the number, breadth, or interconnection of APS
        articles or research projects. The columns are distinct evidential
        categories, not stages through which a domain is expected to progress.
        The coverage families shown here are those identified for the present
        APS general-biological-explanation assessment. They are not intended as
        an exhaustive classification of biology.
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrapper">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Coverage family</th>
              <th scope="col">Principal public APS objects</th>
              <th scope="col">Public coverage</th>
              <th scope="col">Controlled investigation</th>
              <th scope="col">Bounded result</th>
              <th scope="col">Supported local contribution</th>
              <th scope="col">Transfer / broader scope</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Life / biological organisation / organised persistence</td>
              <td>
                <em>Persistence — Organised Continuity Through Time</em>;
                <em>Viability — The Organising Principle of Biological Persistence</em>;
                associated Life and biological-organisation architecture
              </td>
              <td><strong>PRESENT</strong></td>
              <td>
                <strong>CONTROLLED INVESTIGATION</strong> — Life/protocell
                boundary target
              </td>
              <td>
                APS Life criterion was redundant with the strongest comparator
                at the tested boundary target; no additional comparator-relative
                explanatory gain established
              </td>
              <td><strong>NOT ESTABLISHED</strong></td>
              <td><strong>NOT ESTABLISHED</strong></td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td>Agency / function / regulation</td>
              <td>
                <em>Biological Agency as the Activity of Self-Maintenance</em>;
                Function corpus
              </td>
              <td><strong>PRESENT</strong></td>
              <td>
                <strong>CONTROLLED INVESTIGATION</strong> — Agency/<em>E. coli</em>
                chemotaxis and Function/coronary malfunction targets
              </td>
              <td>
                Agency was redundant at its tested target; the Function
                comparison was comparator-favouring
              </td>
              <td><strong>NOT ESTABLISHED</strong></td>
              <td><strong>NOT ESTABLISHED</strong></td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td>Process / Scale / multiscale organisation</td>
              <td>
                APS Process and Scale corpus and core A–P–S architecture
              </td>
              <td><strong>PRESENT</strong></td>
              <td>
                <strong>CONTROLLED INVESTIGATION</strong> — A–P–S/KaiABC and
                A–P–S/axolotl targets
              </td>
              <td>
                Redescription/integrative organisation retained; possible
                heuristic value at the relevant target; additional explanatory
                gain not established
              </td>
              <td><strong>NOT ESTABLISHED</strong></td>
              <td><strong>NOT ESTABLISHED</strong></td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td>Cognition / biological significance</td>
              <td>
                <em>The Cognitive Organisation of Life</em>;
                <em>What APS Means by Cognition</em>; associated cognition corpus
              </td>
              <td><strong>PRESENT</strong></td>
              <td>
                <strong>CONTROLLED INVESTIGATION</strong> — Minimal
                Cognition/<em>C. elegans</em> target
              </td>
              <td>
                Complementarity and classificatory non-redundancy retained;
                additional substantive explanatory gain not established
              </td>
              <td><strong>NOT ESTABLISHED</strong></td>
              <td><strong>NOT ESTABLISHED</strong></td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td>Evolutionary organisation</td>
              <td>
                <em>The Evolutionary Organisation of Life</em>;
                <em>Evolution as the Historical Transformation of Organised Persistence</em>;
                associated Evolutionary Dynamics corpus
              </td>
              <td><strong>PRESENT</strong></td>
              <td><strong>NO QUALIFYING TEST RECORD</strong></td>
              <td>—</td>
              <td><strong>NOT ESTABLISHED</strong></td>
              <td><strong>NOT ESTABLISHED</strong></td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td>Development / repair / regeneration</td>
              <td>
                <em>The Developmental Organisation of Life</em>;
                <em>Repair and Regeneration</em>; associated developmental corpus
              </td>
              <td><strong>PRESENT</strong></td>
              <td>
                <strong>CONTROLLED INVESTIGATION — A–P–S/AXOLOTL REGENERATION
                TARGET ONLY</strong>
              </td>
              <td>
                Integrative redescription retained at the tested target;
                additional explanatory gain not established
              </td>
              <td><strong>NOT ESTABLISHED</strong></td>
              <td><strong>NOT ESTABLISHED</strong></td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td>Ecology / organism–environment relations</td>
              <td>
                <em>The Ecological Organisation of Life</em>;
                <em>Ecology as Organised Persistence Across Scales</em>;
                <em>Ecological Organisation and Organised Persistence</em>
              </td>
              <td><strong>PRESENT</strong></td>
              <td><strong>NO QUALIFYING TEST RECORD</strong></td>
              <td>—</td>
              <td><strong>NOT ESTABLISHED</strong></td>
              <td><strong>NOT ESTABLISHED</strong></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        <strong>No qualifying test record</strong> means that the present GBE
        assessment has not identified a controlled comparative test satisfying
        the evidential requirements used for this matrix. It does not mean that
        the domain has not been researched, that APS makes no claims concerning
        it, or that those claims have been shown to fail.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Not established</strong> records an evidential status; it does
        not mean refuted. Results shown here are target-bound and must not be
        aggregated across rows to infer framework-wide explanatory success or
        failure.
      </p>

      <h2>Featured Research</h2>

      <article class="research-item">
        <h3>
          Agency as the Defining Activity of Life: A Viability-Oriented
          Framework Integrating Process and Scale
        </h3>

        <p><strong>Status:</strong> Peer-reviewed publication</p>

        <p>
          The foundational peer-reviewed presentation of APS as a
          viability-oriented framework for understanding life. It argues that
          biological agency is the defining activity through which living
          organisation sustains itself across process and scale.
        </p>

        <p>
          Spencer, R. D. (2026). <em>Biological Theory</em>.
        </p>

        <p>
          <a
            href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s13752-026-00547-6"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
          >
            DOI
          </a>
          ·
          <a href="https://rdcu.be/fkRpq" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            SharedIt full-text access
          </a>
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="research-item">
        <h3>
          <a href="/articles/comparative-explanatory-methodology-theoretical-biology/">
            Comparative Explanatory Methodology in Theoretical Biology
          </a>
        </h3>

        <p><strong>Status:</strong> Completed APS Research</p>

        <p>
          Establishes a common analytical protocol for comparing
          organisational research programmes through their explanatory
          targets, organising principles, explanatory priorities, conceptual
          architectures, domains of strength, and explanatory scope.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="research-item">
        <h3>
          <a href="/research/auditing-dependency-claims-biological-explanation/">
            Auditing Dependency Claims in Biological Explanation: A Comparative
            Method and Three-Case Test
          </a>
        </h3>

        <p><strong>Status:</strong> Completed APS Research</p>

        <p>
          Develops and tests a relation-sensitive, materially explicit
          procedure for assessing heterogeneous biological dependency claims.
          Its planarian positive result, <em>Pristionchus pacificus</em>
          transfer result, and glucose-homeostasis null test the method’s
          diagnostic capacity, transferability, and principled refusal of
          explanatory gain.
        </p>
      </article>

      <h2>Publications and Preprints</h2>

      <p>
        The APS publication record includes peer-reviewed work, preprints, and
        scholarly papers deposited in recognised repositories. Each output is
        labelled according to its publication and review status.
      </p>

      <p>
        <a href="/publications/">View APS Publications</a>
      </p>

      <h2>Completed APS Research</h2>

      <p>
        Completed APS Research presents substantial, referenced investigations
        developed to scholarly standards and published through APS_WEB.
        Completion indicates that an investigation has reached a stable
        research form; it does not imply external peer review or independent
        confirmation.
      </p>

      <article class="research-item">
        <h3>
          <a href="/articles/comparative-explanatory-methodology-theoretical-biology/">
            Comparative Explanatory Methodology in Theoretical Biology
          </a>
        </h3>

        <p><strong>Status:</strong> Completed APS Research</p>

        <p>
          Develops a disciplined method for comparing biological research
          programmes without reducing their different explanatory priorities
          to a single theory or standard of explanation.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="research-item">
        <h3>
          <a href="/research/auditing-dependency-claims-biological-explanation/">
            Auditing Dependency Claims in Biological Explanation: A Comparative
            Method and Three-Case Test
          </a>
        </h3>

        <p><strong>Status:</strong> Completed APS Research</p>

        <p>
          Applies a common dependency-hypothesis audit to planarian
          regeneration, <em>Pristionchus pacificus</em> mouth-form polyphenism,
          and mammalian glucose homeostasis. The differentiated positive,
          transfer, and null outcomes test whether the procedure changes
          defensible explanatory assessments without manufacturing improvement
          where established accounts are already sufficient.
        </p>
      </article>

      <h2>Research Standards</h2>

      <p>
        APS Research distinguishes established findings from APS
        methodological contributions. Claims are assessed through comparison
        with the strongest available explanations, attention to material
        evidence and inferential limits, consideration of alternatives and
        scope, and explicit failure conditions.
      </p>

      <p>
        Conceptual coherence is not treated as empirical confirmation. APS
        earns scientific credibility only insofar as its explanatory
        connections can be identified, assessed, qualified, revised, or
        rejected.
      </p>

      <h2>Explore the Wider APS Platform</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="/articles/">Articles</a> presents the wider explanatory
          corpus.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/publications/">Publications</a> records formally released
          scholarly outputs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/policies/citation-policy/">Citation Policy</a> explains how
          APS publications, research papers, and website resources should be
          cited.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The APS Framework is the research and explanatory platform through which these
        differentiated resources are organised and made publicly available.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>